title: pidly new
effort: M
area: cli

Allocate the next number, create the directory under the target parent, and
write the four header lines with the schema's required fields stubbed below
the body.

  pidly new [-p parent] [-n "3 8"] <title>     body read from stdin

The next number is the highest anywhere in the tree plus one, closed tickets
included, so there is no counter file. The tree is scanned before the lock is
taken — a stale answer costs only a few extra mkdir attempts and keeps the
critical section short.

Two bugs found by running it. lock() used _n as a spin counter while cmd_new
used _n as the ticket number; POSIX shell has one namespace, so taking the
lock overwrote the allocation and the first ticket in a fresh tracker came
out as 2. Helpers now prefix their locals. And with no wait budget on the
lock, 34 of 40 concurrent creations failed as busy while still producing
correct unique numbers — correct and useless — so new waits where claim does
not.

An open child inside a closed parent is refused rather than silently putting
live work in a finished tree.

Done: implemented in bin/pidly, covered by tests/new.sh (24 cases).
